      <h2>GF Wiki</h2>
      <p>
        This page explains the metrics Generation Football uses alongside
        traditional results: Expected Points (xPts) and Elo ratings.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="#xpoints">Expected Points (xPts)</a>
        ·
        <a href="#elo">Elo ratings</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="xpoints">Expected Points (xPts)</h2>
      <p>
        Expected Points estimate how many points a team
        <em>deserved</em> from a match based on chance quality rather than the
        final score. A team that creates better chances can finish with a high
        xPts total even if the scoreboard does not go their way — and the
        reverse is also true.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each match we take the team’s expected goals (xG) and the
        opponent’s expected goals against them (xGA). We then use a Poisson
        probability model to estimate how likely every realistic scoreline is,
        treating each side’s goals as independent and considering totals from
        0 to 6 goals.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those scoreline probabilities add up to the chances of a win, a draw,
        or a loss. Expected Points are then:
        (win probability × 3) + (draw probability × 1).
      </p>
      <p>
        Season and last-five-match xPts figures are the sum of those match
        values. League tables are still ranked by real points; xPts is there
        to show which teams are over- or under-performing relative to the
        chances they created and conceded.
      </p>

      <h2 id="elo">Elo ratings</h2>
      <p>
        Elo is a running strength rating for teams. We keep
        <strong>club</strong> and <strong>national team</strong> ratings
        separate, so a club’s rating is not mixed with its country’s, and
        matches between clubs and national teams are skipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every team starts at 1500. Ratings use results from the last five
        seasons. Before a match we calculate how likely each side is to take
        points, using the classic Elo formula. Home teams get a +100 rating
        boost, except on designated neutral fixtures (World Cup, Euros and
        similar tournaments, plus club finals), where that advantage is 0.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the match, the result is scored as 1 for a win, 0.5 for a draw,
        and 0 for a loss. Penalty shootouts use the score after extra time, so
        they count as draws. A margin factor then scales the update: 1 for a
        draw or one-goal win, 1.5 for a two-goal win, and
        (11 + goal difference) / 8 for larger margins. The rating change is
        K × margin factor × (actual result − expected result).
      </p>
      <p>
        K depends on how important the match is: 20 for friendlies, 30 for
        domestic league and cup games, 40 for qualifiers and major club
        competitions, 50 for major national-team tournaments, and 60 for the
        World Cup final.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the site, club Elo is the tie-breaker on the
        <a href="/top-teams">Top Teams</a> list after recent form. Team pages
        show the last month of Elo movement, and match pages show how much
        each side gained or lost from that fixture.
      </p>
